===Etymology 1===
[[File:Uncial d.png|class=skin-invert-image|Approximate form of upper case letter D that was the source for lower case d]]
Modification of capital letter {{m|mul|D}}, from {{der|mul|grc|-}} letter {{m|grc|Δ||Delta}}.

====Letter====
{{letter|mul}}

# {{Latn-def|mul|letter|4}}

=====Usage notes=====
The letter {{m|mul|d}} is used in the alphabets of many languages, and in several romanization systems of non-Latin scripts to represent the [[w:Voiced dental and alveolar stops|voiced alveolar or dental plosive]] ({{IPAfont|/d/}}). In some languages and transcription systems, {{m|mul|d}} may also represent other sounds, such as {{IPAfont|/t/}} or {{IPAfont|/ð/}}.

====See also====
{{Latn-script}}
* {{qualifier|select similar letters and symbols}} [[∂]] [[₫]] [[ð]]
* {{qualifier|other scripts}} {{l|el|δ}}, {{l|ru|д}}, {{l|he|ד}}, {{l|ar|sc=Arab|د}}
* See [[Appendix:Variations of "d"]]

===Etymology 2===
Lower case form of upper case roman numeral [[D]], a standardization of {{m|mul|sc=Latn||Ꟈ}} or {{m|mul|sc=Latn||Ð}}, from tally stick markings resembling a superimposed {{m|mul||Ɔ}} and {{m|mul||⋀}}, from the practice of encircling each hundredth {{m|mul||⋀}} notch.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|mul|D}}, {{l|mul|Ⅾ}}, {{l|mul|ⅾ}}

====Numeral====
{{head|mul|numeral|lower case Roman numeral||upper case|D}}

# [[cardinal number]] five [[hundred]] (500).

=====Usage notes=====
With a bar over the numeral, i.e., as d̅, it represents five hundred thousand (500,000).

=====See also=====
* Lesser roman numeral symbol: {{l|mul|c|gloss=100}}
* Greater roman numeral symbol: {{l|mul|m|gloss=1000}}
* [[w:Roman numerals|Roman numerals]]

