=====Usage notes=====
* Sometimes switched with the letter {{angbr|{{l|tl|r}}}} between vowels, {{angbr|w}}, or {{angbr|y}} in a word due to lenition of {{IPAchar|/d/}} to {{IPAchar|/ɾ/}} such as {{m|tl|dadaan}} can become {{m|tl|daraan}}. Compare [[flapping]] in pronunciation of {{ncog|en|medal}}. The letter does not change if the next consonant in the root is an {{IPAchar|/ɾ/}} (such as {{m|tl|madurog}} does not become {{m|tl|marurog}}) or {{IPAchar|/l/}} (such as {{m|tl|madilim}} does not become {{m|tl|marilim}}).
* Some words starting with the letter can also become {{angbr|{{l|tl|r}}}} if the last letter of the preceding word is a vowel, {{angbr|w}}, or {{angbr|y}}. Examples are {{m|tl|daw//raw}}, {{m|tl|dito//rito}}, {{m|tl|dami//rami}}, and {{m|tl|damot//ramot}}.
* On all cases stated above, it is acceptable whether {{angbr|d}} or {{angbr|r}} is used. However, the said phenomena do not occur on proper nouns nor recent loan words.
* In the {{w|Tagalog language#Dialects|Teresa-Morong}} dialect, the letter {{m|tl|r}} may interchange with the letter on any position in the word even when not followed by a vowel, {{angbr|w}}, or {{angbr|y}}. (ex. {{m|tl|ragat//dagat}} and {{m|tl|bayar//bayad}}). Exceptions are recent loanwords, or if the next consonant after a {{IPAchar|/d/}} is {{IPAchar|/ɾ/}} (such as in {{m|tl|durog}}) or {{IPAchar|/l/}} (such as in {{m|tl|dila}}).
* Often switched with the letter {{angbr|r}} after a vowel even on syllable endings in early texts which may indicate ancient pronunciation of words.

